# Score Chart

The dashboard needs a picture of the funnel, not just a table. This file is the
`:chart` unit — a **Svelte client island** that draws a bar chart of lead scores.
It is the first unit in the workbook that runs **in the browser**, so it is worth
being precise about what that means.

## Placement: this runs on the client

Every unit so far ([[score]], [[enrich]], the [[pipeline]]) lives on the **server**
— native Elixir on the BEAM, trusted, allowed to hold secrets and reach the
network through grants. `:chart` is the opposite end of the model. Its `client`
placement word means it ships to the **browser** and runs as **wasm**: the Svelte
component is compiled, the island is hydrated on the page, and from then on it is
just DOM reacting to props. There is no server round-trip to redraw a bar.

That is the whole point of the two-placement model: `client` and `server` are the
only two places code runs, and you choose per unit. We compute scores where the
data and the trust live (server), and we paint pixels where the user is (client).
The weave wires the two together — the bundle that [[weave]] produces carries this
island's compiled code and hydrates it next to the SSR'd page regions.

## Reading data across the boundary

The chart's input is the same `:leads` data the rest of the app uses — typed
values of [[Lead]]'s `Lead` struct. On the client the island receives those
rows as a prop; it does not re-run `query()` or reach back to the server. The
contract is narrow and one-directional: **give me leads, I give you bars**. Each
bar's height is `lead.score` (the integer [[score]] produced on the server), and
its label is `lead.name`. The island never enriches, never scores, never writes —
those are server jobs it has no grant to do.

The `:emit` callback is the one edge that points back out: when the user clicks a
bar, the island emits a `:select` event carrying that lead's `domain`, so the
surrounding dashboard can focus the row. Selection is UI state; it lives here.

![Bar chart of lead scores, Initech tallest](./chart-preview.png)

A bar per lead, sorted high to low — Initech (88) leads, then Acme (70), then
Globex (41). Colour encodes `status`: a `:scored` lead is solid, an `:enriched`
lead that has not been scored yet is hatched, so a glance shows funnel progress.

## The island

The body below is real Svelte — markup, a `<script>`, and scoped `<style>`, exactly
as you'd write a `.svelte` file — set off from the prose by blank lines. The weave
compiles it to a wasm client island and hydrates it on load. Note there is no HTML
page wrapper and no fences here: the `client svelte :chart do` line names the unit,
its placement, its language, and the rest is ordinary component source.

client svelte :chart do
  <script>
    // Props the host passes in. `leads` is an array of Lead-shaped rows
    // ({ name, domain, revenue, employees, score, status }); `emit` lets the
    // island report a selection back to the dashboard region around it.
    export let leads = [];
    export let emit = () => {};

    let selected = null;

    // Sort a copy high-to-low so the tallest bar reads first; never mutate the
    // prop array the host owns.
    $: ranked = [...leads].sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);

    // Scale bar heights to the busiest column. Guard the empty case so we don't
    // divide by zero before the first leads arrive.
    $: top = ranked.reduce((m, l) => Math.max(m, l.score), 0) || 1;

    function height(score) {
      return Math.round((score / top) * 100);
    }

    function pick(lead) {
      selected = lead.domain;
      emit("select", { domain: lead.domain, name: lead.name });
    }
  </script>

  <figure class="chart" role="group" aria-label="Lead scores">
    {#if ranked.length === 0}
      <p class="empty">No leads yet — run the pipeline to populate.</p>
    {:else}
      {#each ranked as lead (lead.domain)}
        <button
          class="bar"
          class:enriched={lead.status === "enriched"}
          class:active={selected === lead.domain}
          style="--h: {height(lead.score)}%"
          title="{lead.name}: {lead.score}"
          on:click={() => pick(lead)}
        >
          <span class="value">{lead.score}</span>
          <span class="track"><span class="fill" /></span>
          <span class="label">{lead.name}</span>
        </button>
      {/each}
    {/if}
  </figure>

  <style>
    .chart {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
      gap: 0.75rem;
      height: 220px;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 1rem 0 0;
    }
    .bar {
      flex: 1 1 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.35rem;
      height: 100%;
      background: none;
      border: 0;
      cursor: pointer;
      font: inherit;
    }
    .value {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      color: var(--ink, #121316);
    }
    .track {
      flex: 1 1 auto;
      width: 100%;
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
    }
    .fill {
      width: 100%;
      height: var(--h);
      border-radius: 6px 6px 0 0;
      background: var(--mint, #aee5c2);
      transition: height 220ms ease, background 120ms ease;
    }
    .bar.enriched .fill {
      background: repeating-linear-gradient(
        45deg,
        var(--mint, #aee5c2),
        var(--mint, #aee5c2) 6px,
        var(--paper, #fbfaf3) 6px,
        var(--paper, #fbfaf3) 12px
      );
    }
    .bar.active .fill {
      background: var(--ink, #121316);
    }
    .label {
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      color: var(--ink-soft, #5a5c61);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .empty {
      margin: auto;
      color: var(--ink-soft, #5a5c61);
    }
  </style>
end

## How it ships in every delivery mode

Because `:chart` is an island, it behaves identically across the three delivery
modes the workbook supports. In a **static bundle** (`salesapp.html`) the
component hydrates from the leads baked into the page. Under **nexus SSR** the
dashboard region renders on the BEAM and the chart hydrates as the one live
island inside it. Under **live SSR** the same island re-receives `leads` as the
server diffs new scores over the RCP websocket — a freshly [[score]]d lead grows
its bar with no reload. One authoring model, three deliveries, no rewrite.

The companion trend view is [[sparkline]] (a SolidJS island), and both sit inside
the page assembled by [[weave]].
